Subject: Re: Matching with replacement and weights
In-Reply-To: <CABo8JBQ3=tQgafkT+PC-8YW5fUFmNPfqS80c4b+dO-q1nuoUng@mail.gmail.com>
References: <4cddac4053ff42418bc5778fcaa6e30b@BN3PR0701MB1622.namprd07.prod.outlook.com> <CANHDHZzWF_J8nuKkfiAyHdr4iHoSoevN-_5y_xwSiX67zm7AiQ@mail.gmail.com> <CABo8JBSGvFtO9ROa-am46qfC+Ee-5n3eyoyZT-eHp7EFA9gVMA@mail.gmail.com> <A7EA4E57-EA53-4BCF-A221-5CA1DCB52F71@jhu.edu> <CABo8JBQ3=tQgafkT+PC-8YW5fUFmNPfqS80c4b+dO-q1nuoUng@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <CD981A71-F921-4DB4-B5C7-EEF36EC85BAF@jhu.edu>

Hi Katerina,
The reason those weights end up being 0.75 and 1.51 is that there aren’t two times as many unmarried people in the control group as in the treatment group; so 2:1 matching isn’t possible within both “married” groups, which means matchit then provides those weights to account for that.  (In other words, because of the exact match, some people end up getting 1 match and others get 2 matches, depending on their marital status), and so then the weights account for that.

> table(lalonde$treat, lalonde$married)

      0   1
  0 209 220
  1 150  35

So short answer is that yes, the weights should definitely be used; otherwise you won’t be reflecting the structure underlying the match and your analyses won’t be accurate.

Liz


